  "reason": "The records collectively establish several relevant ideas: rapid solidification by arc melting on a water-cooled Cu hearth in a closely related Fe-Co-Ni HEA (especially R11), rapid-quench stabilization of multiphase HEAs (R8), composition-controlled dual-phase structures and segregation (R1, R7, R10, R12, R19), and strong sensitivity of magnetism to phase composition and antiferromagnetically aligned constituent moments (R3, R5, R9, R13, R14). Thus an expert could plausibly consider rapid quenching and phase/composition engineering to modify magnetism. However, none identifies FeCoNiTi, an fcc+C14 Laves assemblage, the matched C14-dominant comparison composition, or the specific Ti-sublattice coupling, orbital-moment/spin-canting-assisted domain-wall pinning, and phase assignments of the 168, 214, and 1084 K transitions. The records point in the right general direction but do not make the specific intervention and coercivity mechanism directly reconstructible."
